{"name":"Aurora Borealis","hex":"#556b87","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aurora borealis","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The aurora of the northern hemisphere."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aurora_borealis"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aurora Borealis (painting)","blocks":[{"text":"Aurora Borealis is an 1865 painting by the American artist Frederic Edwin Church of the aurora borealis and the Arctic expedition of Isaac Israel Hayes. The painting measures 142.3 by 212.2 centimetres and is now owned by the Smithsonian American Art Museum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Frederic_Edwin_Church_-_Aurora_Borealis_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Frederic_Edwin_Church_-_Aurora_Borealis_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurora_Borealis_(painting)"},{"title":"Aurora Borealis (icebreaker)","blocks":[{"text":"Aurora Borealis is a proposed European research icebreaker, comparable to the world's strongest icebreakers, planned jointly by a consortium of fifteen participant organizations and companies from ten European nations. If built, she would be the largest icebreaker ever built as well as the first icebreaker built to the highest IACS ice class, Polar Class 1."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/EuropeanResearchIcebreakerAuroraBorealis_LateralCartoon.svg/330px-EuropeanResearchIcebreakerAuroraBorealis_LateralCartoon.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurora_Borealis_(icebreaker)"},{"title":"Aurora","blocks":[{"text":"An aurora is a natural light display in Earth's upper atmosphere caused by charged particles from the Sun colliding with atoms in the atmosphere. These collisions excite oxygen and nitrogen, which then emit light of different colours such as green, red, and purple. When observed in high-latitude regions they are called polar lights and aurora polaris. In the Arctic they are called the northern lights or aurora borealis; in the Antarctic, the term southern lights or aurora australis is used. Auroras display dynamic patterns of radiant light that appear as curtains, rays, spirals or dynamic flickers covering the entire sky."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Aurora_borealis_over_Eielson_Air_Force_Base%2C_Alaska.jpg/330px-Aurora_borealis_over_Eielson_Air_Force_Base%2C_Alaska.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurora"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Frederic_Edwin_Church_-_Rainy_Season_in_the_Tropics_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Frederic_Edwin_Church_-_Rainy_Season_in_the_Tropics_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Frederic_Edwin_Church_-_Rainy_Season_in_the_Tropics_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Frederic_Edwin_Church_-_Rainy_Season_in_the_Tropics_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Frederic_Edwin_Church_-_Rainy_Season_in_the_Tropics_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Frederic_Edwin_Church_-_Rainy_Season_in_the_Tropics_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rainy Season in the Tropics, 1866.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrederic_Edwin_Church_-_Rainy_Season_in_the_Tropics_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Aurora-borealis-model_hg.jpg/500px-Aurora-borealis-model_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Aurora-borealis-model_hg.jpg/1280px-Aurora-borealis-model_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Aurora-borealis-model_hg.jpg/500px-Aurora-borealis-model_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Model of the European research icebreaker Aurora Borealis.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAurora-borealis-model_hg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Flames_in_the_sky.jpg/500px-Flames_in_the_sky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Flames_in_the_sky.jpg/1280px-Flames_in_the_sky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Flames_in_the_sky.jpg/500px-Flames_in_the_sky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlames_in_the_sky.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_15.jpg/500px-G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_15.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_15.jpg/1280px-G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_15.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_15.jpg/500px-G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_15.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AG5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_15.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Aurora_australis_ISS.jpg/500px-Aurora_australis_ISS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Aurora_australis_ISS.jpg/500px-Aurora_australis_ISS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAurora_australis_ISS.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Antarctic_aurora_ESA313457.jpg/500px-Antarctic_aurora_ESA313457.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Antarctic_aurora_ESA313457.jpg/1280px-Antarctic_aurora_ESA313457.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Antarctic_aurora_ESA313457.jpg/500px-Antarctic_aurora_ESA313457.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Earth's night-side upper atmosphere appears from the bottom as bands of afterglow illuminating the troposphere in orange with silhouettes of clouds at the top, and the stratosphere in white and blue at the top of the middle. Next, the mesosphere (pink area) extends to the orange and faintly green line of the lowest airglow, at about one hundred kilometres at the edge of space and the lower edge of the thermosphere (invisible). Continuing with green and red bands of auroras stretching over several hundred kilometres.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntarctic_aurora_ESA313457.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Aurora_shapes.jpg/500px-Aurora_shapes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Aurora_shapes.jpg/1280px-Aurora_shapes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm